module

-- **Yours (H).** Hand-written, and hand-written *by you*: it arrives as a stub
-- or a `sorry`, and nothing will fill it in. A signature install leaves it
-- alone, so what is put here is kept.
--
-- ###########################################################################
-- ## THE CALCULUS-SPECIFIC SEAM. START HERE.                               ##
-- ###########################################################################
--
-- `Proofs/Checker.lean` -- the ~25 preservation theorems and the soundness
-- theorem -- is *not* calculus-specific. In Logos it is byte-identical between
-- `Cpc` (591 rules) and `CpcMini` (5 rules), two packages that differ in rule
-- set, in signature, *and* in which invariants their rules need. It names no
-- rule and no operator.
--
-- What differs between those two packages is this file.
--
-- The core checker maintains three invariants of the proof state on its own:
-- everything on the stack is well typed, translatable, and locally true. If
-- your rules need something more of the state than that, it goes in the slot
-- below, and `Checker.lean` is written against these four names without ever
-- mentioning what they stand for.
--
-- **A calculus that needs nothing extra leaves this as it is** -- pointed at
-- `True` -- and pays nothing for it. That is what `CpcMini` does.
--
-- In CPC the slot holds *variable stability*, which exists so that
-- binder-sensitive rules (`instantiate`, `skolemize`, `alpha_equiv`) can be
-- given premise truth in a variable-variant model. If your calculus has such
-- rules, this is where that lives.
--
-- **This choice is coupled to `Proofs/RuleSupport/Support.lean`.** Making the
-- extra invariant load-bearing means adding a corresponding field to the
-- evidence a rule receives about its premises -- in Logos, `true_in_var_model`
-- alongside `true_here`. The two are decided together, up front, and are
-- awkward to separate later.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What Logos does here
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- `Cpc/Proofs/Invariants/Stability.lean`, 552 lines against CpcMini's 488 --
-- and the difference is not duplication: CpcMini defines
-- `StableWhenTrueInAnyVarModel` as `True` and pays nothing, while Cpc points
-- the slot at a real invariant.
--
-- CPC's invariant is *assumption stability*: every assumption on the checker
-- stack stays true when the model's variable assignment is varied. Written out,
-- it is a walk over the stack asserting `StableWhenTrueInAnyVarModel` of each
-- `assume` and `assume_push` entry and nothing of `proven` entries.
--
-- **The cost is not in this file.** `StableWhenTrueInAnyVarModel` is defined in
-- `Cpc/Proofs/Closed/Support.lean`, and `Closed/` is **32,274 lines** -- larger
-- than the entire checker layer. CpcMini has none of it.
--
-- **And exactly one rule uses the slot.** Of 591 CPC rules, `Re_unfold_neg` is
-- the only one whose proof reaches for `true_in_var_model`. (The `Closed/`
-- machinery beneath is shared more widely -- `Quant_unused_vars` and other
-- binder rules import it directly -- but the *invariant* has a single
-- consumer.)
--
-- Two things to take from that. Leaving this at `True` is the common case and
-- costs nothing, so do not reach for it speculatively. And when one rule does
-- need it, the price is paid in the machinery it rests on rather than here --
-- so the question to ask is not "do I want this invariant" but "am I prepared
-- to build what it stands on".
public import @CALCULUS@.Proofs.Assumptions
import all @CALCULUS@.Proofs.Assumptions

@[expose] public section

open Eo
open Smtm

namespace Eo

/--
Whatever your rules need of the proof state beyond well-typed, translatable and
locally true.

`True` if they need nothing.
-/
abbrev checkerExtraInvariant (_M : SmtModel) (_s : CState) : Prop := True

/-- What a single command must satisfy for the extra invariant to survive it. -/
abbrev cmdExtraOk (_M : SmtModel) (_c : CCmd) : Prop := True

/-- The same over a command list. -/
abbrev CmdListExtraOk (_M : SmtModel) (_cs : CCmdList) : Prop := True

/-- What the input assumptions must satisfy for the extra invariant to hold initially. -/
abbrev extraAssumptionListOk (_M : SmtModel) (_F : Term) : Prop := True

/--
Running a command preserves the extra invariant.

The one obligation the slot carries. `Checker.lean` applies it and never looks
inside; with the definitions above at `True` it is immediate, and it becomes
real work exactly when they do.
-/
theorem invoke_cmd_preserves_extraInvariant_nonstuck
    (M : SmtModel) (s : CState) (c : CCmd) :
    s ≠ CState.Stuck ->
    cmdExtraOk M c ->
    checkerExtraInvariant M s ->
    checkerExtraInvariant M (__eo_invoke_cmd s c) := by
  intro _ _ _
  trivial

end Eo
